Static design closure before the prototype:

- Scheduler wake identity is the next committed `EntryVersion` for Resolve,
  small-cycle Verify, any-cycle Verify and Ready. `EntryVersion` is globally
  unique, so a changed non-empty head is an allocation-free progress edge;
  an unchanged tail insertion does not need another hint because the existing
  head already owns a stored/waiting hint and its checkout changes the head.
- Resolve cannot share one wake-one signal indiscriminately with verifiers. A
  verifier may consume that hint and correctly prefer Verify work, leaving the
  Resolve head unchanged. The sealed topology therefore has three role hints:
  dedicated resolver, small verifier and any verifier. A Resolve-head change
  notifies all three; a small-Verify change notifies small and any as selected
  by their exact scheduler heads; a large-Verify change notifies any. The
  dedicated resolver makes helper consumption unable to strand Resolve.
- Ready, dependency maintenance and effect publication each have one consumer
  and use one stored wake-one hint. Dependency publication needs only the
  empty-to-nonempty edge because the sole driver continues after every bounded
  successful step. Remote and Accepted expiry retain the independent one-second
  wall-clock tick.
- Effect-capacity release is not a work hint. Service, Ready, maintenance and
  compute paths must return a typed `WaitEffectCapacity` step and subscribe
  before their attempt. A release broadcasts because waiter effect classes and
  batch byte sizes differ; wake-one cannot prove that the selected waiter fits.
- The effect publisher wakes only when checkout becomes possible or closed and
  drained becomes observable. Template lanes retain their local candidate-uncle
  wake and receive an authority broadcast only when the exact pool template
  source versions change; their current source-cut OCC remains unchanged.
- `PreparedApply::apply` is the sole composite before/after wake compiler. Its
  helper functions return retirement data, and the outer exhaustive match adds
  one `AuthorityWakeTransition`. This avoids a per-transition caller map.
- `CommittedDelta::into_post_commit` explicitly destroys removals, owners,
  replaced effect storage and generations after the authority guard opens,
  then returns a move-only post-commit receipt. `AuthoritySignals` consumes
  that receipt, publishes timing observations and routes derived hints. The
  checkout-plus-capture-failure path composes consecutive transitions so no
  intermediate under-lock level is published. Notification from a lock-guard
  destructor remains forbidden because it would precede retirement.
- Production consumers have been enumerated: retained/direct ingress,
  administration, ordered chain Apply, expiry/dependency maintenance, compute
  checkout/settlement/cancellation, Ready, effect checkout/settlement/close,
  effect-capacity retry loops and the five existing template tasks. Scratch
  generation construction and unit fixtures deliberately discard wake receipts
  because they expose no runtime consumer.

